export const DATA_43: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-051", num: 51, title: "Đề 051", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Benefits of Regular Physical Exercise", paragraphs: ["Most doctors and health professionals agree that regular physical exercise is one of the most important things a person can do for their overall health. Whether it is a brisk morning walk, a swim at the local pool, a cycling session, or a team sport, physical activity benefits the body and mind in numerous ways. Despite this widespread agreement, a large number of people around the world do not exercise enough. Busy schedules, sedentary jobs, and the increasing availability of screen-based entertainment have made it easy to spend long hours sitting down without engaging in meaningful physical activity. Understanding the full range of benefits that exercise provides may help motivate more people to build regular movement into their daily lives.", "The most well-known benefit of regular exercise is its positive effect on physical health. Cardiovascular exercise — activities that raise the heart rate, such as jogging, swimming, or cycling — strengthens the heart muscle and improves the efficiency of the circulatory system. People who exercise regularly have a lower risk of developing serious health conditions such as heart disease, type 2 diabetes, and high blood pressure. Exercise also helps to maintain a healthy body weight by increasing the number of calories the body burns both during and after physical activity. Additionally, weight-bearing exercises such as walking and strength training help to build and maintain bone density, reducing the risk of osteoporosis in later life.", "Beyond physical health, exercise has a profound and well-documented effect on mental wellbeing. When the body engages in physical activity, the brain releases chemicals called endorphins — natural substances that produce feelings of happiness and reduce the perception of pain. This is sometimes referred to as the \"runner's high\", though the effect is not limited to running and can be triggered by many forms of exercise. Regular physical activity has also been shown to reduce symptoms of anxiety and depression, improve sleep quality, and boost overall mood. For people dealing with chronic stress, exercise provides a healthy and effective outlet for tension that can be difficult to manage through other means.", "Exercise is also important for maintaining cognitive function across the lifespan. Research has shown that physical activity increases blood flow to the brain, promotes the growth of new brain cells, and supports the connections between them . These effects are associated with improvements in memory, concentration, and problem-solving ability. In older adults, regular exercise has been linked to a slower rate of cognitive decline and a reduced risk of developing conditions such as dementia and Alzheimer's disease. In children and younger adults, studies have found that those who exercise regularly tend to perform better academically than those who lead more sedentary lives.", "Social interaction is another benefit that exercise can provide, particularly for people who participate in group activities or team sports. Joining a running club, attending a fitness class, or playing on a sports team creates opportunities to meet new people, build friendships, and develop a sense of belonging. For many individuals, the social element of exercise is one of the strongest motivators for maintaining a regular routine. Knowing that others are expecting you to show up for a group workout can be a powerful incentive that is difficult to replicate when exercising alone.", "Despite all these advantages, many people find it challenging to make exercise a consistent part of their lives. Common barriers include lack of time, physical discomfort, low motivation, and uncertainty about where to begin. Health experts recommend that adults aim for at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity exercise per week — a target that does not need to be reached all at once but can be broken down into shorter sessions spread across the week. Starting with small, manageable goals and gradually increasing intensity and duration is widely considered the most sustainable approach. The most important first step, according to most health professionals, is simply to start moving — and to find a form of exercise that feels enjoyable enough to continue."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["The relationship between sedentary behaviour and the rising global rates of heart disease and diabetes", "The many physical, mental, cognitive, and social benefits of regular exercise, and why people struggle to exercise consistently", "Why team sports are more effective than individual exercise for improving long-term health outcomes", "How to design an exercise programme suitable for people of all fitness levels"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following health conditions can regular exercise help to prevent?", options: ["Migraines and tension headaches", "Asthma and other respiratory conditions", "Insomnia and chronic fatigue syndrome", "Type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what are endorphins?", options: ["Brain chemicals produced during exercise that create feelings of happiness and reduce pain", "Vitamins stored in the muscles that are released during intense physical activity", "Proteins that help repair muscle tissue after strenuous physical activity", "Hormones that regulate the body's heart rate and breathing during cardiovascular exercise"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what cognitive benefit of exercise has been observed in older adults?", options: ["A slower rate of cognitive decline and a reduced risk of developing dementia", "An improved ability to multitask, which reduces the risk of workplace accidents", "A complete reversal of memory loss caused by age-related brain conditions", "A significantly higher ability to learn new languages compared to those who do not exercise"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"sustainable\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["scientifically proven through large-scale clinical trials", "affordable and accessible to people of all income levels", "able to be maintained consistently over a long period of time", "environmentally friendly and low in carbon emissions"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how much moderate-intensity exercise do health experts recommend for adults each week?", options: ["At least 150 minutes, which can be spread across multiple shorter sessions", "A minimum of 200 minutes, to be achieved gradually over several months", "At least 60 minutes, completed in a single continuous session", "A minimum of 100 minutes, divided into no more than three sessions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["older adults who benefit most from regular exercise", "health conditions such as dementia and Alzheimer's disease", "researchers who study the relationship between exercise and brain health", "new brain cells whose connections are supported by physical activity"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"incentive\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a formal obligation agreed upon between members of a group", "an official reward given for achieving a specific fitness goal", "a social event organised to celebrate a group's progress", "something that motivates or encourages a person to take action"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a physical health benefit of regular exercise?", options: ["A reduced risk of developing heart disease", "Greater bone density that reduces the risk of osteoporosis", "A lower likelihood of developing skin conditions caused by poor circulation", "Improved efficiency of the circulatory system"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 5 about the advantages of group exercise over exercising alone?", options: ["People who exercise in groups are less likely to suffer injuries than those who train alone", "Exercising with others is only beneficial for people who already have high levels of physical fitness", "Group exercise always produces faster physical results than individual training programmes", "The social accountability of group exercise provides a stronger motivation to maintain a regular routine"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Evolution of E-Learning in Modern Education", paragraphs: ["The delivery of education through digital technology has undergone a transformation so rapid and so sweeping that it has fundamentally altered the relationship between learners, teachers, and knowledge itself. What began in the 1990s as a modest experiment in delivering course materials via the early internet has, within three decades, grown into a global industry worth hundreds of billions of dollars, enrolling hundreds of millions of learners across every continent. The emergence of massive open online courses, interactive learning management systems, and AI-driven personalisation tools has created an educational landscape that would have been unrecognisable to educators working at the dawn of the digital age. Understanding how e-learning evolved, what it has achieved, and where its limits lie is essential for anyone seeking to navigate the future of education.", "The earliest forms of e-learning were relatively rudimentary by today's standards. Distance education programmes in the late twentieth century typically involved the delivery of static text documents, pre-recorded video lectures, and basic multiple-choice assessments through institutional websites or CD-ROM packages. Interaction between students and instructors was limited to email correspondence and asynchronous discussion forums. Despite these constraints, early adopters quickly recognised that digital delivery offered something traditional education struggled to provide at scale: flexibility. Learners could access course materials at any hour, from any location with internet connectivity, without the need to attend scheduled classes in a fixed physical space.", "The transformative shift came in the early 2010s with the rise of Massive Open Online Courses, or MOOCs. Platforms such as Coursera, edX, and Udacity partnered with prestigious universities including Harvard, MIT, and Stanford to offer free or low-cost courses to anyone with an internet connection. Within months of their launch, some courses had attracted enrolments in the hundreds of thousands — figures that dwarfed the capacity of even the largest physical lecture halls. Advocates celebrated MOOCs as a democratising force, capable of delivering world-class instruction to learners in remote villages as readily as to those in well-resourced urban centres. Critics, however, were quick to flag a structural weakness: completion rates for MOOCs were alarmingly low, with most studies finding that fewer than ten percent of enrolled students finished the courses they started.", "The problem of learner engagement and completion has driven much of the subsequent innovation in e-learning design. Researchers identified that the passive consumption of video lectures — the dominant format of early MOOCs — was poorly suited to sustaining motivation over the duration of a full course. In response, instructional designers began incorporating a range of interactive elements: spaced repetition exercises, peer-assessed assignments, gamification features such as points and leaderboards, and live virtual sessions that allowed students to interact with instructors and peers in real time. These design changes have demonstrably improved retention and completion rates in many contexts, though a significant gap between enrolment and completion persists across the sector as a whole.", "The integration of artificial intelligence into e-learning platforms has opened a further dimension of possibility. Adaptive learning systems, which continuously analyse each learner's performance data and adjust the content, pacing, and difficulty of instruction accordingly, have shown considerable promise in delivering personalised learning experiences at scale. Unlike a traditional classroom, where a single teacher must simultaneously address the needs of thirty or more students with varying levels of ability and prior knowledge, an adaptive AI system can, in principle, provide each learner with a customised pathway through the curriculum. The practical limitations of these systems — including the quality of their training data, the narrowness of the skills they can reliably assess, and concerns about the privacy implications of continuous behavioural monitoring — mean that their deployment requires careful management and ongoing evaluation.", "Access and equity remain the most persistent challenges confronting the e-learning sector. The optimistic narrative of digital education as a universal leveller has been complicated by the stark reality of the global digital divide. Learners in high-income countries with reliable broadband access, modern devices, and strong digital literacy skills are far better positioned to benefit from e-learning than those in low-income regions where connectivity is unreliable, devices are shared or unavailable, and the cultural and linguistic assumptions embedded in most e-learning content reflect the priorities of a narrow range of predominantly English-speaking, Western educational institutions. Several ambitious international programmes have attempted to address these disparities, but the gap between the populations most in need of alternative educational pathways and those most able to use e-learning effectively remains a defining limitation of the sector.", "The COVID-19 pandemic served as an inadvertent and unprecedented global stress test for e-learning infrastructure. When school and university closures forced entire national educational systems online virtually overnight, the limitations of existing digital provision were exposed with painful clarity alongside its possibilities. Systems in which e-learning had been carefully planned and resourced adapted with relative success; those that had treated digital learning as a peripheral supplement to conventional schooling found themselves profoundly underprepared. The legacy of this experience has been a widespread recognition that e-learning is not a substitute for well-resourced in-person education, but rather an indispensable complement to it — a tool whose value depends entirely on the quality of planning, training, and institutional commitment that surrounds its use."], questions: [{ q: "What is the primary purpose of the passage?", options: ["To compare the educational outcomes of students in high-income and low-income countries who use e-learning platforms", "To argue that e-learning is superior to traditional classroom education in all major respects", "To trace the evolution of e-learning, examine its achievements and shortcomings, and assess its role alongside conventional education", "To explain why completion rates for online courses remain low despite significant investment in e-learning platforms"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was one key advantage that early e-learning offered over traditional education?", options: ["The ability to connect learners directly with expert instructors anywhere in the world via live video", "A wider range of interactive assessment tools that gave students instant, detailed feedback", "A more rigorous academic curriculum that was developed in partnership with leading universities", "Flexibility in when and where learners could access course materials, without fixed class times"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what significant problem did research reveal about MOOCs?", options: ["The courses attracted mainly learners from wealthy countries, failing to reach those in remote communities", "The partnership between platforms and universities led to conflicts over course content and ownership", "Most enrolled students did not complete the courses they signed up for, with completion rates below ten percent", "The courses were too difficult for most learners without prior university-level education"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following design changes was introduced to improve learner engagement in e-learning?", options: ["Requiring learners to pass a pre-course test before accessing the main learning content", "Incorporating gamification elements such as points and leaderboards alongside peer-assessed tasks", "Replacing video lectures with text-based reading materials to reduce screen fatigue", "Limiting course enrolment to smaller groups to create a more personalised experience"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one practical limitation of adaptive AI learning systems?", options: ["They are too expensive for most educational institutions to deploy at a meaningful scale", "They require learners to have advanced technical skills in order to operate them effectively", "They can only function effectively when learners have access to high-speed internet connections", "Their ability to reliably assess skills is narrow, and concerns exist about continuous behavioural monitoring"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a barrier preventing equal access to e-learning globally?", options: ["E-learning content that reflects the priorities of predominantly English-speaking Western institutions", "Unreliable internet connectivity in low-income regions", "A shortage of qualified instructors capable of designing effective online courses", "Limited availability of modern devices in many communities"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"rudimentary\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["highly sophisticated and technically advanced", "carefully structured according to established pedagogical theory", "deliberately simplified to suit beginner learners", "basic and undeveloped compared to later standards"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"flag\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["draw attention to or identify as a concern", "exaggerate for rhetorical effect", "resolve or eliminate", "formally document in an official report"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"its\" in the last paragraph refers to ____", options: ["e-learning", "institutional commitment", "in-person education", "digital technology"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the future role of e-learning in education?", options: ["The effectiveness of e-learning will continue to depend on how thoughtfully it is planned, resourced, and integrated alongside human teaching", "E-learning is best suited to adult and professional learners and will never be fully effective for younger students", "E-learning will eventually make physical schools and universities unnecessary as connectivity improves globally", "Advances in AI will resolve all current limitations of e-learning, including low completion rates and the digital divide"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Emergence of the Subscription Economy", paragraphs: ["Over the past two decades, a fundamental shift has taken place in the way goods and services are bought and sold. Where consumers once purchased products outright — a CD, a software licence, a newspaper subscription — they now increasingly pay recurring fees for ongoing access to digital content, software platforms, curated goods, and even physical products delivered to their doors on a regular schedule. This model, broadly referred to as the subscription economy, has grown from a niche arrangement associated primarily with print media into a dominant commercial paradigm that now shapes industries ranging from entertainment and software to healthcare and personal finance. [1] Analysts estimate that the global subscription economy has expanded by more than 435 percent over the past decade, a pace of growth that has fundamentally altered the strategic priorities of businesses and the consumption habits of hundreds of millions of people worldwide.", "The appeal of the subscription model to businesses is readily understood. Recurring revenue streams provide a degree of financial predictability that one-time sales cannot match, making it easier to forecast cash flow, plan long-term investment, and satisfy the expectations of shareholders and investors. Subscription models also create powerful incentives for companies to invest in customer retention rather than purely in customer acquisition — a shift that many strategists argue produces better products and stronger service quality over time. Furthermore, the continuous relationship between provider and subscriber generates rich behavioural data that companies can use to refine their offerings, personalise recommendations, and identify patterns of engagement that inform future product development.", "From the consumer's perspective, the subscription model carries its own set of attractions and drawbacks. On the positive side, subscriptions typically offer access to a broad range of content or services for a predictable monthly or annual fee, eliminating the need for large upfront purchases and providing flexibility to cancel or modify arrangements as needs change. Streaming platforms, for instance, grant access to libraries of thousands of films and television programmes for a fee that would once have purchased a small handful of physical media. [2] For software products in particular, subscriptions have shifted the burden of maintenance and updates from the individual user to the provider, ensuring that subscribers always have access to the most current version of a product without incurring additional costs.", "The drawbacks, however, are equally real. Consumer advocates have raised concerns about what researchers have termed \"subscription fatigue\" — the growing sense among consumers that the cumulative cost of maintaining multiple subscriptions across entertainment, productivity, health, and lifestyle categories is becoming difficult to track and increasingly burdensome. Studies in several countries have found that consumers consistently underestimate their total monthly subscription expenditure, sometimes by substantial margins. This cognitive difficulty in aggregating dispersed recurring charges is considered a structural feature of the subscription model rather than an incidental side effect, and critics argue that companies deliberately exploit it by setting subscription fees at price points designed to fall below the threshold of active financial attention.", "The subscription economy has also generated broader market structure concerns. As subscription-based platforms have grown, several sectors have experienced significant consolidation, with dominant players leveraging their subscriber bases and data advantages to acquire or outcompete smaller rivals. [3] In the music streaming industry, for example, a small number of platforms now control the overwhelming majority of paid subscriptions globally, creating a distribution bottleneck that affects the royalty earnings of independent artists and the diversity of content that reaches mainstream audiences. Regulators in multiple jurisdictions have begun examining whether the competitive dynamics of subscription-dominated markets require new frameworks for assessing market concentration and protecting consumer choice.", "A further dimension of the subscription economy that has attracted growing academic interest is its psychological dimension. Research in behavioural economics suggests that the subscription model exploits several cognitive tendencies, including loss aversion — the well-documented human preference for avoiding losses over acquiring equivalent gains — to retain subscribers who might otherwise cancel. Platforms have been criticised for deploying so-called \"dark patterns\" in their cancellation processes: deliberately complex menus, obscured confirmation steps, and strategically timed retention offers designed to introduce friction into the decision to end a subscription. [4] Regulators in the European Union and the United States have begun targeting these practices, with some jurisdictions now requiring that cancellation be made as simple as the original sign-up process.", "The long-term trajectory of the subscription economy remains genuinely uncertain. On one hand, the model's advantages for both businesses and consumers in certain contexts appear durable, and the infrastructure of recurring billing, data analytics, and personalised content delivery is now deeply embedded in the global digital economy. On the other hand, the accumulation of consumer frustrations, regulatory interventions, and evidence of market concentration suggests that the model as currently practised may be approaching a point of renegotiation — one at which companies that prioritise genuine value delivery over retention-by-friction will be better positioned to earn lasting consumer loyalty than those that depend on inertia to sustain their revenue streams."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the main concern of the passage?", options: ["An argument that the subscription economy is harmful to consumers and should be more strictly regulated by governments", "An examination of the subscription economy's growth, its advantages and drawbacks for businesses and consumers, and the market and regulatory concerns it has raised", "A comparison of different subscription pricing strategies used by companies in the entertainment and software industries", "A historical account of how print media subscriptions evolved into the digital streaming services dominant today"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what advantage does the subscription model offer businesses beyond financial predictability?", options: ["It allows companies to increase prices each year without triggering significant customer cancellations", "It eliminates the need for companies to invest in new product development once a subscriber base is established", "It generates continuous behavioural data that can be used to improve offerings and personalise recommendations", "It reduces the need to comply with consumer protection regulations that apply to one-time purchases"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what specific benefit does the subscription model offer consumers of software products?", options: ["Subscribers are entitled to receive physical copies of software alongside digital access", "Software subscriptions are always less expensive than the equivalent one-time purchase price", "The responsibility for maintenance and updates passes to the provider, ensuring access to the most current version", "Subscribers can share their software access across an unlimited number of devices and users"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have studies found regarding consumers and their subscription expenditure?", options: ["Most consumers cancel subscriptions within three months of signing up due to dissatisfaction", "Consumers deliberately maintain more subscriptions than they use to avoid missing out on services", "Consumers consistently underestimate their total monthly subscription spending, often by significant amounts", "Subscription costs have risen so rapidly that most consumers now spend more on subscriptions than on food"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 4 and 5, which of the following best describes two distinct concerns raised about the subscription economy?", options: ["The lack of variety in subscription content libraries and the decline of physical retail caused by subscription delivery services", "The difficulty consumers face in cancelling subscriptions and the inability of small companies to adopt subscription billing technology", "The psychological exploitation of consumers through hidden fee structures and the environmental impact of digital infrastructure", "The tendency for consumers to underestimate cumulative subscription costs and the consolidation of markets around dominant subscription platforms"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a specific \"dark pattern\" tactic used by subscription platforms?", options: ["Deliberately complex cancellation menus", "Strategically timed retention offers designed to interrupt cancellation", "Obscured confirmation steps during the cancellation process", "Automatically upgrading subscribers to more expensive service tiers without consent"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 4 in the phrase \"critics argue that companies deliberately exploit it\" refers to _______.", options: ["The cognitive difficulty consumers have in mentally totalling dispersed recurring charges", "The regulatory gap that prevents authorities from enforcing transparent billing practices", "The growing variety of subscription categories spanning entertainment, health, and lifestyle", "The tendency for subscription fees to be set at artificially low introductory rates"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"refine\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To expand the range of products offered to an existing customer base", "To improve or make more precise through adjustment and analysis", "To replace an existing product with an entirely new version", "To restrict access to services based on a subscriber's engagement patterns"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"aggregating\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Comparing the value of different subscription tiers against one another", "Identifying which subscription charges are legitimate and which are fraudulent", "Collecting and combining separate amounts into a single total", "Negotiating lower fees across multiple subscription services simultaneously"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Today, subscription arrangements extend to areas as unexpected as motor vehicles, household appliances, and even prescription eyewear, reflecting how thoroughly the model has migrated beyond its digital origins.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Great Deep-Sea Mining Debate", paragraphs: ["At depths of between 4,000 and 6,000 metres beneath the ocean surface, scattered across vast areas of the abyssal plain, lie polymetallic nodules — potato-sized mineral concretions containing commercially significant concentrations of manganese, nickel, cobalt, and copper. [1] These same metals are central to the manufacture of lithium-ion batteries and other components of the clean energy transition, a coincidence of demand and supply that has transformed what was once a theoretical extractive frontier into an arena of urgent commercial and geopolitical interest. The prospect of harvesting these nodules at industrial scale has generated one of the most contested resource governance debates of the contemporary period — a debate in which the stakes are defined not merely by economic value, but by the fate of ecosystems that remain, in significant part, scientifically unmapped.", "The institutional framework governing deep-sea mineral extraction is centred on the International Seabed Authority, or ISA, an intergovernmental body established under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea to regulate mining activity in international waters beyond national jurisdiction. Since the early 2010s, the ISA has issued dozens of exploration licences to state-sponsored and private entities across a number of designated zones, most notably the Clarion-Clipperton Zone — a fracture zone in the central Pacific Ocean estimated to contain nodule deposits of extraordinary density and extent. The Authority is formally mandated to ensure that exploitation activities are conducted in a manner that prevents serious harm to the marine environment, a mandate whose practical meaning has become the central point of contention in regulatory negotiations.", "Proponents of deep-sea mining advance several interconnected arguments in support of commercial extraction. Chief among these arguments is the metals demand argument: the global transition away from fossil fuels requires enormous quantities of cobalt, nickel, and manganese for battery storage systems, and terrestrial mining of them carries its own well-documented environmental and human rights costs, including habitat destruction, toxic tailings, and the use of artisanal labour in dangerous conditions in some producing nations. [2] Advocates contend that harvesting polymetallic nodules from the abyssal plain may, on a life-cycle basis, generate a smaller ecological footprint than equivalent terrestrial extraction — a claim supported by some preliminary modelling but contested by marine ecologists who question whether the abyssal environment's extreme sensitivity has been adequately factored into such comparisons.", "The environmental case against proceeding with commercial deep-sea mining is grounded primarily in the irreversibility argument. The abyssal ecosystems in which nodule deposits are concentrated are among the least disturbed habitats on the planet, characterised by extraordinarily slow biological processes — nodule formation itself proceeds at a rate of only a few millimetres per million years — and by endemic species whose existence beyond localised populations is unknown. Sediment plumes generated by collection vehicles traversing the seabed would disperse across hundreds of kilometres, smothering filter-feeding organisms and disrupting food chains whose structure and resilience are poorly understood. Critically, because recovery timescales for such ecosystems likely extend across centuries or millennia, the damage from even a single large-scale extraction event may be functionally permanent on any human time horizon.", "A further dimension of the opposition concerns scientific knowledge gaps. A significant proportion of the species inhabiting the proposed mining zones have not yet been formally described, and baseline ecological surveys — necessary to assess impact and monitor change — remain incomplete for most licensed areas. [3] Marine biologists have argued that proceeding with extraction before adequate baseline data exists violates a fundamental principle of environmental risk management: that the burden of proof for the safety of a proposed activity should rest with those who seek to undertake it, not with those who raise objections to it. Several prominent research institutions and a growing coalition of Pacific Island nations have formally called for a moratorium on commercial extraction pending the completion of comprehensive environmental assessments.", "The governance debate at the ISA has itself become a source of controversy. The pace at which the Authority issued exploration licences during the 2010s drew criticism from environmental observers who argued that commercial momentum was being allowed to shape regulatory design rather than the reverse. More recently, the invocation by a Canadian mining company of a provision in the ISA's founding treaty — allowing a sponsoring state to trigger a two-year countdown toward commercial extraction even in the absence of finalised mining regulations — created significant diplomatic and legal turbulence within the organisation. [4] The episode exposed what critics described as a structural tension within the ISA's dual mandate: to both facilitate the orderly development of seabed resources and to protect the marine environment — objectives that may, under conditions of commercial pressure, prove fundamentally incompatible.", "What distinguishes the deep-sea mining debate from many previous resource extraction controversies is the degree to which it is, at its core, a dispute about epistemic standards — about how much we need to know before we are entitled to act, and who bears the cost when the knowledge required to act responsibly has not yet been produced. The ocean floor is not simply a remote location; it is a repository of biological and chemical complexity whose full significance to planetary systems — including carbon cycling, nutrient dynamics, and climate regulation — is only beginning to be understood. Whether the world's demand for clean energy materials justifies proceeding under conditions of irreducible uncertainty is a question whose answer will say as much about the values of the present generation as about the technical merits of extraction itself."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following most accurately describes the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Deep-sea mining should be halted immediately because the environmental damage it causes is already irreversible", "The International Seabed Authority has successfully balanced commercial development with environmental protection in its management of deep-sea mineral licences", "The demand for metals driven by the clean energy transition makes deep-sea mining an economic and environmental necessity that outweighs its ecological risks", "The deep-sea mining debate raises complex questions about resource governance, environmental irreversibility, scientific uncertainty, and the standards of knowledge required before extractive activity can proceed responsibly"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the primary role of the International Seabed Authority?", options: ["To set global prices for polymetallic nodules and distribute extraction revenues to developing nations", "To adjudicate disputes between nation-states over the ownership of deep-sea mineral deposits", "To regulate mining activity in international seabed areas and ensure that exploitation does not cause serious harm to the marine environment", "To fund scientific research into the biodiversity of abyssal ecosystems before any extraction takes place"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, on what basis do proponents argue that deep-sea nodule harvesting may be preferable to terrestrial mining?", options: ["Abyssal ecosystems contain no known species of conservation concern, making environmental impact negligible", "Deep-sea mining technology is more advanced and less expensive than the equipment used in terrestrial mineral extraction", "On a life-cycle basis, harvesting nodules may produce a smaller ecological footprint than equivalent land-based extraction, which carries documented environmental and human rights costs", "Deep-sea nodules contain higher concentrations of commercially valuable metals than any known terrestrial deposit"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the passage describe potential damage from deep-sea mining as \"functionally permanent on any human time horizon\"?", options: ["The species living in abyssal zones reproduce too slowly to sustain population losses caused by industrial extraction", "Recovery timescales for abyssal ecosystems likely extend across centuries or millennia, meaning damage from a single large-scale extraction event may never be reversed within a humanly meaningful timeframe", "Mining vehicles permanently alter the chemical composition of seawater, making recovery of marine life biologically impossible", "International law prohibits any restoration activity in international waters, leaving damaged ecosystems without legal recourse"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 4 and 5, which of the following best represents two distinct environmental objections to deep-sea mining raised in the passage?", options: ["The irreversibility of damage to slow-recovering abyssal ecosystems and the absence of adequate baseline scientific data needed to assess and monitor extraction impacts", "The noise pollution generated by extraction equipment and the legal ambiguity surrounding ownership of species discovered in licensed mining zones", "The likelihood of accidental oil spills from mining vessels and the disruption to submarine cable networks used for global communications", "The risk of radioactive contamination from nodule processing facilities and the threat to coastal fishing communities dependent on deep-sea species"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the opposition to proceeding with commercial deep-sea mining?", options: ["International legal proceedings brought against mining companies for violations of marine environmental law", "The incompleteness of baseline ecological surveys in licensed mining zones", "The argument that those seeking to extract should bear the burden of proving safety", "Formal calls for a moratorium by Pacific Island nations and research institutions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 3 in the phrase \"the global transition...requires enormous quantities of cobalt, nickel, and manganese for battery storage systems, and terrestrial mining of these metals carries its own...costs\" refers to _______.", options: ["The polymetallic nodules scattered across the abyssal plain", "The cobalt, nickel, and manganese needed for clean energy manufacturing", "The battery storage systems required by the clean energy transition", "The artisanal labourers working in dangerous conditions in producing nations"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"mandate\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A binding target set by an international treaty for the reduction of environmental harm", "A formal protest lodged by member states against a proposed regulatory decision", "A financial agreement between a regulatory body and the companies it oversees", "An official authority or duty assigned to an organisation to carry out a specific function"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"resilience\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The rate at which an ecosystem accumulates biomass under stable environmental conditions", "The capacity of a system to recover its structure and function after being disturbed", "The biological diversity of a habitat as measured by the number of species it contains", "The degree to which an organism can adapt its behaviour in response to changing food sources"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'The surge in demand from electric vehicle manufacturers and grid-scale battery producers has transformed this calculus dramatically, lending commercial urgency to extraction proposals that might otherwise have remained indefinitely in the planning phase.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-057", num: 57, title: "Đề 057", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Challenge of Modern Independence", paragraphs: ["Growing up and becoming independent is one of the most important journeys a person takes in their lifetime. For many young adults, the move away from family — whether to study at university, start a new job, or simply live on their own for the first time — is an exciting but also challenging experience. Learning to manage your own life without the constant support of parents or guardians requires new skills, a new mindset, and a great deal of patience with yourself.", "One of the first challenges that many young people face when living independently is managing money. When you live at home, many costs are covered by your family — food, electricity, rent, and household supplies. Once you move out, however, all of these expenses become your responsibility. Budgeting, which means planning how to spend and save your money carefully, is a skill that takes time to develop. Many young adults admit that they spend too much in the first few months of living alone and then struggle to pay for basic necessities later in the month. Financial experts recommend writing down all your income and expenses each week so that you can see clearly where your money is going and make adjustments when needed.", "Cooking is another area where many young people feel unprepared. At home, meals are often prepared by parents, and young people may not have learned even the most basic cooking techniques. When living alone, eating out at restaurants every day quickly becomes too expensive, so learning to cook simple, healthy meals is an important step toward true independence. Fortunately, there are many free resources available online — including video tutorials and recipe websites — that make it easy to learn how to prepare a variety of dishes without any previous experience. Starting with simple recipes and gradually attempting more complex ones is a practical approach that builds both skill and confidence in the kitchen.", "Beyond practical skills, living independently also means taking full responsibility for your own schedule and daily habits. Without a parent to remind you to wake up on time, go to bed early, or complete important tasks, it is easy for daily routines to fall apart. This can lead to problems such as missing deadlines, feeling disorganised, or becoming overtired. Setting a regular daily routine — including fixed times for waking up, working or studying, exercising, and sleeping — helps to create a sense of structure and control. Many young adults find that writing a simple daily plan the night before helps them stay focused and productive throughout the following day.", "Another common difficulty is dealing with feelings of loneliness. Moving away from family and childhood friends can feel isolating, especially during the first weeks in a new place. It is normal to miss the comfort and familiarity of home. However, loneliness does not have to be a long-term problem. Joining clubs, attending community events, or simply introducing yourself to neighbours are all good ways to start building a new social network. Making new friends takes time, but most people find that once they begin making an effort to connect with others, the feeling of isolation gradually fades.", "Finally, it is important to remember that asking for help is not a sign of weakness. Many young people feel pressure to appear completely self-sufficient and capable, and as a result, they avoid asking for advice or support when they need it. In reality, everyone — no matter how experienced or independent — sometimes needs guidance. Whether it is calling a parent to ask how to fix something around the house, speaking to a financial advisor about managing debt, or talking to a counsellor about feelings of stress, seeking help when needed is one of the most mature things a person can do.", "The path to independence is rarely smooth, and it is perfectly normal to make mistakes along the way. Every challenge faced and overcome — whether it is cooking a proper meal, managing a monthly budget, or simply surviving a difficult week alone — adds to a young person's growing sense of capability and self-confidence. With time, patience, and a willingness to keep learning, modern independence becomes not just a challenge to endure, but a rewarding way of life to embrace."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To compare the lifestyles of young adults who live alone with those who live with family", "To explain why financial management is the most important skill for independent living", "To describe the various challenges of living independently and offer practical advice for overcoming them", "To argue that young people should not leave home until they are fully prepared"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do financial experts recommend to help young adults manage their money?", options: ["Avoiding any large purchases during the first year of independent living", "Asking parents to transfer money each month until stable income is established", "Opening a separate savings account before moving out of the family home", "Writing down all income and expenses each week to track spending clearly"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what resources does the passage suggest young people can use to learn how to cook?", options: ["Printed cookbooks available at public libraries", "Cooking classes offered by local community centres", "Advice from neighbours who enjoy preparing food", "Free online video tutorials and recipe websites"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a possible result of not having a regular daily routine?", options: ["Missing important deadlines and feeling disorganised", "Developing poor cooking habits due to irregular mealtimes", "Spending too much money on food and entertainment", "Losing contact with family members and close friends"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what approach does the passage recommend for dealing with loneliness after moving to a new place?", options: ["Returning home to visit family as frequently as possible", "Spending more time on social media to maintain existing friendships", "Taking active steps such as joining clubs and attending community events", "Waiting patiently until new neighbours introduce themselves first"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way to build a new social network?", options: ["Calling family members regularly", "Joining local clubs", "Introducing yourself to neighbours", "Attending community events"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The advice to set fixed times for waking up and going to sleep", "The breakdown of daily routines when there is no one to give reminders", "The habit of writing a daily plan the night before", "The difficulty of completing important tasks at university or work"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"mature\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Having many years of experience in independent living", "Feeling confident enough to live without any support", "Showing good judgement and responsible behaviour", "Physically grown to full adult size"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"adjustments\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Changes made to improve or correct a situation", "Calculations used to predict future income and expenses", "Strict rules that must be followed every day without exception", "Agreements reached between two people about shared costs"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraphs 6 and 7 about the author's view of making mistakes during independent living?", options: ["The author suggests that mistakes are embarrassing and should be hidden from family", "The author argues that the only way to avoid mistakes is to seek professional advice before moving out", "The author believes that making mistakes proves a young person is not ready to live alone", "The author implies that early difficulties and errors are a natural and even valuable part of building self-confidence"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Understanding and Relieving Psychological Stress", paragraphs: ["Stress is one of the most common experiences in modern life. From demanding workloads and financial pressures to relationship difficulties and health concerns, people of all ages encounter situations that place strain on their mental and emotional resources. While a small amount of stress can be beneficial — motivating people to meet deadlines, prepare for challenges, or take necessary action — chronic stress, when it persists over long periods without adequate relief, poses a serious risk to both mental and physical health.", "Psychologists define stress as the body's response to any demand or threat, whether real or perceived. When a person encounters a stressful situation, the brain signals the release of hormones such as adrenaline and cortisol into the bloodstream. These hormones prepare the body for immediate action by increasing heart rate, sharpening focus, and redirecting energy away from non-essential functions. This automatic response, often called the \"fight-or-flight\" response, was essential for early humans who faced physical dangers in their environment. In the context of modern life, however, the same response is triggered by psychological pressures — such as a difficult conversation, an overdue task, or financial uncertainty — that do not require a physical reaction, leaving the body in a prolonged state of tension.", "The physical consequences of chronic stress are well-documented and far-reaching. Sustained elevation of cortisol levels has been linked to a weakened immune system, high blood pressure, disrupted sleep patterns, and an increased risk of cardiovascular disease. Over time, stress can also contribute to digestive problems, headaches, and muscle tension. Perhaps most significantly, chronic stress is strongly associated with mental health conditions such as anxiety disorders and depression. Research consistently shows that individuals who experience high levels of stress over extended periods are significantly more likely to develop these conditions than those who maintain effective stress management practices.", "Despite these well-established risks, many people find it difficult to recognise when their stress levels have become harmful. One reason for this is that the gradual build-up of stress can feel normal, particularly in environments where overwork or constant busyness is treated as a sign of productivity or dedication. A person may adapt to a high-stress lifestyle and only realise the toll it has taken when physical or emotional symptoms become impossible to ignore. Health professionals therefore encourage people to regularly check in with themselves — noticing changes in sleep quality, appetite, mood, and energy levels — as early warning signals that stress management may be needed.", "Fortunately, a wide range of strategies exists for managing and reducing psychological stress. Physical exercise is among the most consistently recommended, as it triggers the release of endorphins — natural mood-lifting chemicals in the brain — and provides an immediate outlet for physical tension. Regular aerobic activities such as walking, cycling, or swimming have been shown to reduce overall cortisol levels and improve resilience to future stressors. Mindfulness practices, including meditation and controlled breathing exercises, have also gained considerable scientific support, helping individuals develop the ability to observe stressful thoughts without being overwhelmed by them .", "Social connection is another powerful buffer against stress. Talking to a trusted friend, family member, or mental health professional about sources of stress can provide both emotional relief and practical perspective. Studies have found that individuals with strong social support networks recover from stressful events more quickly and report lower overall levels of psychological distress. Importantly, this benefit does not require extensive social contact — even brief, meaningful interactions with others can activate the biological systems that counteract the stress response and promote a sense of calm and safety.", "Time management and the setting of realistic priorities are also widely recommended as preventive tools. A significant source of stress for many people is the feeling of being overwhelmed by too many demands at once. Breaking large tasks into smaller, manageable steps, setting clear boundaries between work and personal time, and learning to say no to commitments that exceed one's capacity are all practical ways to reduce the accumulation of unnecessary pressure. When combined with adequate rest, a nutritious diet, and regular engagement with enjoyable activities, these approaches can substantially reduce a person's overall susceptibility to stress and improve their long-term quality of life."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["An explanation of what psychological stress is, how it affects health, and strategies for managing it", "The argument that modern lifestyles are the primary cause of mental health problems worldwide", "A comparison of different types of stress experienced by people at different stages of life", "The history of medical research into the effects of stress on the human body"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happens in the body when a person encounters a stressful situation?", options: ["Blood pressure drops sharply to prevent the heart from overworking", "Hormones such as adrenaline and cortisol are released, increasing heart rate and sharpening focus", "The brain enters a resting state to conserve energy for later use", "The digestive system speeds up to provide extra energy for physical activity"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a physical health consequence of chronic stress?", options: ["Increased production of endorphins that temporarily improve mood", "Improved sleep quality due to elevated hormone levels at night", "A weakened immune system and higher risk of cardiovascular disease", "Faster recovery from illness because of a more active immune response"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do many people fail to recognise when their stress has become harmful?", options: ["They are unaware that stress can have any negative health consequences", "The gradual build-up of stress can feel normal, especially in environments that reward constant busyness", "Medical tests are required to detect harmful stress levels, and most people do not take them", "Stress symptoms only appear suddenly, making them impossible to predict"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what finding about social support networks is mentioned in the passage?", options: ["Long daily conversations with friends are the most effective way to reduce cortisol levels", "Social support is only effective when provided by trained mental health professionals", "Individuals with strong social support recover more quickly from stressful events and report lower psychological distress", "People with wide social networks are immune to the negative effects of stress"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a recommended method for managing stress?", options: ["Mindfulness meditation and controlled breathing", "Keeping a personal journal to record daily worries", "Observing stressful thoughts without being overwhelmed by them", "Regular aerobic exercise such as walking or cycling"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["Health professionals who treat stress-related conditions", "Psychologists who study the relationship between stress and illness", "People who maintain effective stress management practices", "Individuals who have already developed anxiety or depression"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"beneficial\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Normal and expected in everyday situations", "Useful or having a positive effect", "Difficult to manage without professional support", "Harmful to health over extended periods of time"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"susceptibility\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The level of awareness a person has about their own mental health", "The likelihood of being easily affected or harmed by something", "The ability to recover quickly after a stressful experience", "The determination to continue working despite feeling stressed"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the possibility of managing psychological stress?", options: ["Neutral, presenting only scientific facts without any guidance or recommendations", "Practical and encouraging, indicating that a range of effective strategies is available to most people", "Pessimistic, suggesting that stress is an unavoidable condition that cannot be meaningfully reduced", "Critical, implying that current medical treatments for stress are inadequate and unreliable"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Global Rise of Myopia", paragraphs: ["Over the past five decades, myopia — commonly known as short-sightedness — has shifted from a relatively contained optical condition to what researchers now describe as a global epidemic. [1] In East Asian countries such as China, South Korea, and Singapore, studies estimate that between 80 and 90 percent of young adults completing secondary education are myopic, a figure that represents a dramatic increase from rates recorded a generation ago. In Western nations, the prevalence has roughly doubled since the 1970s. The sheer speed of this change has convinced researchers that genetic factors alone cannot account for the trend, directing scientific attention firmly toward environmental and behavioural causes.", "Myopia occurs when the eyeball grows slightly too long from front to back, causing light to focus in front of the retina rather than directly on it. The result is that distant objects appear blurred while close objects remain clear. In mild cases, the condition is a manageable inconvenience correctable with glasses or contact lenses. In more severe forms, however, high myopia significantly raises the risk of serious complications including retinal detachment, glaucoma, and macular degeneration — conditions that can lead to permanent vision loss. The growing proportion of young people developing high myopia, rather than the mild variety, has added a layer of urgency to what might otherwise seem a routine optical concern.", "Among the environmental factors under investigation, two have attracted particular scientific attention: near-work activities and reduced outdoor time. Near-work refers to any visual task performed at close range, such as reading, writing, or using digital screens. For decades, it was assumed that the strain of prolonged near-work was the primary driver of myopia progression. More recent research, however, has complicated this picture considerably. Studies comparing children with similar amounts of near-work but different levels of outdoor activity have found that those who spend more time outside develop myopia at significantly lower rates — a finding that has shifted the emphasis of the debate from what children are doing indoors to what they are missing when they stay inside.", "The mechanism through which outdoor time may protect against myopia development is not yet fully understood, but the leading hypothesis centres on light intensity. Natural daylight is many times brighter than typical indoor lighting, and exposure to high-intensity light is believed to stimulate the release of dopamine in the retina. Dopamine, in turn, appears to inhibit the excessive axial elongation of the eyeball — the physical change that underlies myopia. This hypothesis is supported by animal studies and a growing body of human data, though researchers caution that the precise pathways involved are still being mapped and that additional mechanisms may also play a role.", "Responses to the myopia epidemic have varied considerably across different countries, reflecting both the severity of the problem and the degree of public health infrastructure available to address it. Taiwan was among the first jurisdictions to implement a national outdoor time policy for schoolchildren, mandating at least 120 minutes of outdoor activity per school day. [2] Early data from this programme suggested a measurable slowing in the rate of myopia progression among participating students. China subsequently launched large-scale trials of similar interventions, and several European countries have begun exploring comparable policies. Alongside behavioural interventions, optical and pharmaceutical approaches — including specially designed contact lenses and low-dose atropine eye drops — have shown promise in clinical trials as tools for slowing myopia progression, though their long-term safety profiles are still under evaluation.", "Critics of the outdoor time hypothesis, however, point to confounding variables that complicate the interpretation of existing data. Children who spend more time outdoors may also engage in more physical activity, experience less academic pressure, or come from socioeconomic backgrounds that differ systematically from those of their more sedentary peers. Disentangling the specific contribution of light exposure from these associated factors remains a methodological challenge for researchers. [3] Furthermore, while national outdoor time mandates are relatively straightforward to implement in school settings, persuading families to reduce screen time and increase unsupervised outdoor play in the home environment has proved considerably more difficult in practice.", "What is clear is that the rise of myopia is not a self-correcting trend. Without deliberate intervention at both individual and systemic levels, prevalence rates are projected to continue climbing. [4] Some forecasters estimate that by 2050, approximately half of the world's population may be myopic, with a significant proportion affected by high myopia. The implications for global health systems — in terms of the cost of corrective eyewear, the burden of treating myopia-related complications, and the potential impact on workforce productivity — are substantial enough to warrant the kind of coordinated international response that has, thus far, remained largely absent."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the main focus of the passage?", options: ["An examination of the global rise of myopia, its causes, health consequences, and the responses it has prompted", "An argument that genetic factors are more important than environmental ones in explaining myopia rates", "A detailed guide to the optical mechanisms through which myopia develops in children", "A comparison of myopia treatment methods currently available in different countries"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has the increase in high myopia rates been treated with particular concern?", options: ["High myopia is now more common than mild myopia in all countries studied", "High myopia significantly raises the risk of serious complications that may result in permanent vision loss", "High myopia cannot be corrected with conventional glasses or contact lenses", "Researchers have found that high myopia develops only in children under the age of ten"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 3 and 4, what has recent research revealed about the relationship between outdoor time and myopia?", options: ["Children who spend more time outdoors perform better academically, which reduces eye strain from near-work", "Outdoor time reduces the total number of hours children spend on near-work activities each day", "Prolonged outdoor time has been conclusively proven to reverse myopia that has already developed", "Children who spend more time outside develop myopia at lower rates, possibly due to the stimulating effect of natural light intensity on retinal dopamine"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what role does dopamine play in the leading hypothesis about outdoor light and myopia?", options: ["It regulates the production of melanin in the iris, which filters harmful light wavelengths", "It increases the sensitivity of the retina to low-light conditions, reducing eye strain indoors", "It improves the focusing ability of the lens, helping the eye adjust between near and far distances", "It is believed to slow or prevent the excessive lengthening of the eyeball that causes myopia"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the outdoor time policy implemented by Taiwan for schoolchildren?", options: ["A minimum of 90 minutes of combined indoor and outdoor physical activity per day", "At least 120 minutes of outdoor activity mandated during each school day", "A minimum of 60 minutes of outdoor activity per school day", "Two hours of screen-free time required each evening after school"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a measure that has been trialled or adopted to address myopia progression?", options: ["Specially designed contact lenses", "Mandatory reductions in formal classroom hours", "Low-dose atropine eye drops", "National outdoor time mandates in schools"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["Schools that have introduced outdoor time mandates for their students", "Near-work activities that are considered most damaging to developing eyes", "Researchers who study the relationship between screen time and eyeball development", "Children in the comparison group who spend more time outdoors"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"inhibit\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To monitor and record changes in a physical structure", "To prevent or slow down a process from occurring", "To accelerate or intensify a biological process", "To trigger the release of a chemical within a cell"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"confounding\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Arising unexpectedly during a controlled laboratory experiment", "Supporting the main findings of a scientific study", "Contradicting the central hypothesis of a research programme", "Introducing complications that make accurate interpretation more difficult"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'In some of the worst-affected urban centres, optometrists report that the majority of children are already presenting with measurable myopia before they reach secondary school.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Cultural and Economic Impact of SEA Games Side Events", paragraphs: ["The Southeast Asian Games, held biennially among the eleven member nations of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, has long been understood primarily through the lens of athletic competition. Yet the event's significance extends well beyond the medal tally. [1] In recent editions, the portfolio of activities surrounding the main sporting programme — collectively referred to as side events — has expanded substantially, encompassing cultural festivals, trade exhibitions, tourism showcases, and diplomatic receptions. This broadening of scope has prompted researchers, economists, and policymakers to examine the side events not as peripheral additions to the sporting spectacle, but as substantive instruments of soft power, cultural diplomacy, and economic development in their own right.", "The cultural programming associated with the SEA Games has historically served as a platform for host nations to project a carefully curated image of national identity to both regional and international audiences. Traditional performing arts, culinary exhibitions, craft markets, and heritage tourism experiences are typically woven into the event calendar, offering visitors and broadcast audiences exposure to aspects of the host country's culture that would not ordinarily feature in standard tourism promotion. The curation of this cultural content is rarely incidental; host organising committees invest considerable resources in selecting which traditions, art forms, and narratives to foreground, making the cultural programme as much an exercise in nation-branding as in cultural celebration.", "The economic dimensions of SEA Games side events are multifaceted and not always straightforwardly positive. On the revenue side, trade fairs and business forums attached to the Games have demonstrated measurable capacity to accelerate bilateral and multilateral commercial relationships among member states. Vietnam's hosting of the 2003 and 2021 editions, for example, was accompanied by investment promotion conferences that attracted significant foreign interest and are credited by some analysts with contributing to a broader upturn in inbound foreign direct investment in the periods that followed. The tourism multiplier effect — whereby spending by visiting athletes, officials, and spectators generates downstream economic activity in hospitality, retail, and transport sectors — has also been documented, though economists caution that isolating this effect from broader economic trends requires careful methodological design.", "The costs and risks associated with hosting, however, are equally significant and are sometimes underweighted in pre-event projections. Infrastructure expenditure — including the construction or renovation of sporting venues, transport links, and accommodation facilities — can impose substantial fiscal burdens on host governments, particularly those in lower-income member states. The risk of infrastructure becoming redundant after the Games concludes — the so-called \"white elephant\" problem familiar from larger international sporting events — is a genuine concern. Several host cities in the region have faced criticism in the years following their Games for venues that fell into disuse, representing a misallocation of public resources that might have been directed toward more enduring development priorities.", "The diplomatic function of side events is perhaps the least quantifiable but arguably the most consequential dimension of the SEA Games experience. Informal meetings between heads of government, ministerial-level bilateral discussions, and structured dialogues on regional issues — including trade, environmental cooperation, and security — have historically taken place on the sidelines of the Games. [2] Scholars of regional integration argue that these encounters, precisely because they occur within a celebratory rather than a formal diplomatic setting, allow for a degree of candour and relationship-building that more structured forums may not easily facilitate. Whether this diplomatic value can be attributed specifically to the side event programme or simply to the co-location of regional leaders is a question that resists easy resolution.", "Critics of the expanding side event apparatus contend that the proliferation of non-sporting activities risks diluting the Games' identity as an athletic competition and diverting organisational resources away from the core sporting programme. [3] There is also a concern that commercially driven trade exhibitions and investment forums serve the interests of large corporations and government-linked enterprises disproportionately, while offering little tangible benefit to the local communities in whose neighbourhoods the Games are physically staged. This tension between the macro-economic rationale for side events and their micro-level distributional effects remains an unresolved fault line in the literature on mega-event legacies.", "Looking ahead, the trajectory of SEA Games side events is likely to be shaped by two countervailing pressures: the growing appetite among host nations for the soft-power and economic dividends that an expanded programme can deliver, and the increasing scrutiny applied by civil society and independent economists to the actual versus projected returns on event-related public investment. [4] How individual host nations navigate this tension — whether by scaling back ambitions, improving evaluation frameworks, or pursuing more community-centred models of event design — will determine whether the side event dimension of the SEA Games evolves into a genuinely sustainable instrument of regional development or remains, as its critics suggest, a costly exercise in elite-driven spectacle."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following most accurately captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["SEA Games side events are primarily valuable as tools of cultural diplomacy and should be expanded in future editions", "SEA Games side events have been widely criticised by member nations and are likely to be reduced in scope in future editions", "The financial costs of hosting SEA Games side events consistently outweigh the cultural and diplomatic benefits they generate", "The cultural and economic dimensions of SEA Games side events are significant but involve genuine trade-offs and unresolved tensions that warrant careful evaluation"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the passage suggest about the cultural programming of SEA Games side events?", options: ["Host nations focus exclusively on traditional performing arts and exclude contemporary cultural forms from the programme", "Cultural programmes are assembled without central coordination, reflecting the spontaneous contributions of local communities", "It is designed primarily to entertain visiting athletes rather than to promote the host nation's identity", "The selection of cultural content is deliberate and functions as an exercise in national image-building as much as in cultural celebration"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how are Vietnam's 2003 and 2021 editions of the SEA Games referenced in the passage?", options: ["As evidence that trade fairs attached to the Games can accelerate bilateral commercial relationships", "As the first editions in which side events were formally recognised as part of the official Games programme", "As cases where investment promotion conferences contributed to measurable increases in foreign direct investment", "As cautionary examples of host nations that overspent on sporting infrastructure"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the \"white elephant\" problem as described in the passage?", options: ["The problem of host nations overpromising cultural programmes that cannot be delivered within budget", "The difficulty of attracting sufficient numbers of international spectators to justify the cost of hosting the Games", "The risk that infrastructure built for the Games becomes unused after the event ends, representing a misuse of public funds", "The tendency for diplomatic agreements reached during the Games to be abandoned shortly afterwards"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do scholars of regional integration consider the diplomatic side events particularly valuable?", options: ["The diplomatic discussions held during the Games are legally binding under ASEAN protocols", "They are the only occasions on which all eleven ASEAN member nations are represented at head-of-government level", "Side event diplomacy has historically led to the resolution of longstanding territorial disputes between member states", "The celebratory setting allows for a degree of openness and relationship-building that more formal diplomatic forums may not easily facilitate"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a criticism of the expanding side event programme?", options: ["The diversion of organisational resources away from the core sporting programme", "The disproportionate benefit to large corporations over local communities", "The risk of diluting the Games' identity as an athletic competition", "The potential for side events to create diplomatic tensions between member states"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 5 in the phrase \"Whether this diplomatic value can be attributed specifically to the side event programme\" refers to _______.", options: ["The value generated by the cultural performances and craft exhibitions held alongside the sporting events", "The economic multiplier effect produced by the presence of government delegations at host-city hotels and restaurants", "The diplomatic benefit arising from informal meetings and bilateral dialogues that occur in the celebratory setting of the Games", "The formal trade agreements signed between member states during the Games"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"curation\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The formal preservation of endangered cultural practices through government funding", "The process by which international audiences assess the authenticity of a host nation's cultural output", "The deliberate selection and arrangement of content to create a particular impression or narrative", "The spontaneous emergence of cultural traditions during large public gatherings"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"redundant\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Poorly maintained as a result of insufficient government funding", "Technically outdated due to advances in construction methods", "No longer needed or useful for its original purpose", "Excessively expensive relative to the benefits provided"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Advocacy groups and independent audit bodies in several member states have begun demanding more rigorous pre-event cost-benefit analyses and post-event impact assessments as a condition of public support for future bids.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
